/**
@defgroup structs any CBOR into a C++ struct and back

`cbor::structs<T>` writes a C++ value as one CBOR item. It reads one CBOR
item back into a value of type `T`. Include `<cbor/cbor.hpp>`.

A struct is written as a map. Each public data member is one entry, and
its key is the name of the member. A description changes the keys, adds a
tag, or names the members of a class. See KEYS AND DESCRIPTIONS.

`cbor::structs` finds the members of an aggregate by reflection. On g++ 16
with `-std=c++26 -freflection`, the members come from reflection. On every
other compiler, Boost.PFR 1.84 or later finds them. If the system has no
Boost.PFR, the build uses the copy that the library ships. A class with a
base class, and a class that is not an aggregate, needs a description.
Every translation unit of a program must see the same Boost.PFR.

@section structs_encode WRITE A VALUE

`cbor::structs<T>::encode(value)` gives `std::expected<std::string,
cbor::error>`, the bytes of the item.

`cbor::structs<T>::encode(value, target)` appends the bytes to `target`. It
gives `std::expected<std::size_t, cbor::error>`, the count of bytes that it
appended. The target is a `std::string`, a container of `char`, a
`std::span<char>`, or a writer of the program. A `std::span<char>` that is
too small for the bytes gives `no_buffer_space`.

The keys of a struct come in the order of RFC 8949 section 4.2.1. The
functions write no shared value (tag 28 and tag 29).

The encoder writes a float or a double in the shortest width that holds its
value exactly (RFC 8949 section 4.1). A NaN keeps its bits:

- A double NaN takes the binary16 width when the low 42 bits of its
  significand are zero. It takes the binary32 width when the low 29 bits are
  zero. Else it takes the binary64 width.
- A float NaN takes the binary16 width when the low 13 bits of its
  significand are zero. Else it takes the binary32 width.

@section structs_decode READ A VALUE

`cbor::structs<T>::%decode` reads one CBOR item. It does not read a byte
after the item. It gives `std::expected<cbor::ref<T>, cbor::error>`. The
ref holds the owner of the bytes. `*result` is the ref, and `**result` is
the value of `T`. A `std::string_view` or a `std::span` in `T` points into
the bytes. It stays valid while the ref lives. No form copies the bytes.

The forms are:

- `%decode(std::move(encoded))`: `encoded` is an rvalue of `std::string` or
  of another owning range of `char`. The bytes move into a new owner. An
  lvalue does not compile.
- `%decode(owner)`: `owner` is a `std::shared_ptr` to a range of `char`. The
  result keeps the owner.
- `%decode(owner, target)`: the read puts the values into the containers of
  `target`, and it uses their allocator.
- `%decode(owner, part)`: `part` is a `std::string_view` into memory that
  `owner`, a `std::shared_ptr<void const>`, keeps alive.

The forms without `target` start from `T{}`, so `T` must be default
constructible. A null or empty owner throws `std::logic_error`.

A `target` of `std::pmr` containers sets the memory resource of each
element. When the resource cannot give memory, the read gives `not_enough_memory`. The decoder
reserves no memory for a count that the input states. It makes each element
when it reads that element.

@section structs_types TYPES

`T`, and each member of a struct in `T`, has one of these C++ types. The
CBOR item is on the right:

- `bool`: `true` or `false`.
- `std::nullptr_t`: `null`. `cbor::simple_value`: a simple value.
- An integer type of 8 to 64 bits: an integer. Where the compiler has
  `__int128`, that type and its unsigned form give an integer or a bignum
  (tag 2 or 3).
- An enum with a fixed underlying type: the integer of that type. `%decode`
  does not compile for an enum with no fixed underlying type, because some
  values of its underlying type are not values of the enum.
- `float` and `double`: the shortest float that holds the value exactly.
  `%decode` takes a binary16, binary32 or binary64 item. A binary64 item that
  a `float` cannot hold exactly gives `number_out_of_range`.
- `std::string`, `std::pmr::string` and `std::string_view`: a text string.
- `std::span<std::byte const>`: a byte string.
- `std::vector` or `std::array` of `std::byte`, of an integer of 8 to 64
  bits, of `float` or of `double`: a typed array of RFC 8746, in little
  endian. The tag is the one of the element type. For example, tag 64 is
  for `std::byte` and `std::uint8_t`. `%decode` takes this item only. A plain
  array gives `incorrect_type`.
- `std::set` and `std::flat_set`: tag 258 on an array, as the CBOR sets
  specification defines it. `%decode` takes the items in any order.
- `std::chrono::sys_seconds`: tag 1 on an integer of seconds since
  1970-01-01. `%decode` also takes a float whose value is a whole number.
- `std::chrono::year_month_day`: tag 100 on an integer of days since
  1970-01-01 (RFC 8943). `encode` throws `std::logic_error` for a date that
  is not valid.
- Where the standard library has `std::chrono::parse`, `%decode` also takes
  the date-time string of tag 0 for a `sys_time`, and the full-date string
  of tag 1004 for a `year_month_day`.
- `std::optional<E>`: the item of `E`, or `null` when the optional is empty.
  An empty optional member has no key in the map.
- `std::variant<E...>`: the item of the alternative that the variant holds.
  `%decode` gives the first alternative that accepts the item. An integer
  alternative accepts an integer that it can hold. With
  `std::variant<std::uint8_t, std::int64_t, std::string>`, the item 256 gives
  the `std::int64_t` alternative.
- `std::tuple<E...>` and `std::array<E, N>` of other types: an array of
  exactly that count.
- `std::map`, `std::unordered_map` and their `std::pmr` forms: a map.
  `std::multimap` and `std::unordered_multimap`: a map from each key to the
  array of its values. RFC 8949 section 5.6 makes a map with a repeated key
  invalid.
- `std::vector`, `std::deque`, `std::list` and other containers: an array.
- `cbor::tagged_item<N, E>`: tag N on the item of `E`. `E` is not a struct.
- A struct: a map. See KEYS AND DESCRIPTIONS.

@section structs_keys KEYS AND DESCRIPTIONS

The key of a member is its name, as a text string. A description sets
another key, or a tag. Each macro writes its description at global scope,
after the type and after the description of each struct that the type
holds. A description that no call uses is not checked.

- `CBOR_MEMBER(T, m)`: the entry of the member `m` of `T`. The key is the
  text of the name `m`.
- `CBOR_DEFINE_TYPE_MEMBERS(T, tag, m...)`: the description of `T` from the
  names of its members. Each key is the name of its member. The list takes
  up to 343 names.
- `CBOR_DEFINE_TYPE(T, tag, entries...)`: the description of `T` from
  entries. Each entry is `cbor::member{key, &T::m}`. A key is a text string,
  or an integer from -2^63 to 2^64 - 1. No C++ literal holds -2^63. Use
  `std::numeric_limits<std::int64_t>::min()` for that key.

The tag is `cbor::tag{n}`, or `cbor::untagged` for a map with no tag. A
struct whose description has a tag carries that tag. `%decode` gives
`incorrect_type` when the tag is missing or is another tag. An untagged
struct gives `incorrect_type` for a tag in front of its map.

The rules for a description are these:

- Each entry names a public, non-static data member of the type, or of a
  public base class.
- With reflection, the entries are exactly the public data members of the
  type and of its public bases. Another list does not compile.
- A member that comes twice, two members with one key, a key of `bool` or
  of a character type, a tag of a standard type (for example 1, 258 or a tag
  of RFC 8746), and an invalid tag do not compile.
- An annotation `[[=cbor::key("x")]]` or `[[=cbor::tag(n)]]` on a type that
  `structs` reads does not compile.
- A class with a base class uses `CBOR_DEFINE_DERIVED_TYPE` or
  `CBOR_DEFINE_DERIVED_TYPE_MEMBERS`. These take the base class after the
  type.
- A type whose name holds a comma needs a type alias for the macros.
- A class with no default constructor cannot use the forms without
  `target`. It decodes into the target that the caller gives: `%decode(owner,
  target)`.

@section structs_input INPUT FROM OTHER PROGRAMS

`%decode` reads the entries of a map in any order. It skips an entry whose
key no member has, whatever the type of its value. So a program reads the
bytes of a newer program that added members. A new member of type
`std::optional` also reads the bytes of an older program.

@section structs_shared SHARED VALUES

`%decode` reads tag 28 and tag 29 of value sharing (cbor.schmorp.de). It uses
a table that lasts for one call. `encode` writes no shared value.

- A tag 28 is allowed on each member. It keeps the value.
- No member takes a tag 29. A tag 29 gives an error:
  `sharedref_index_not_marked` when no tag 28 comes before the index of the
  tag 29; `sharedref_not_complete` when that tag 28 is in a member that
  `%decode` skips; `incorrect_type` for any other tag 29.

@section structs_errors ERRORS

Each function gives a `cbor::error` in its `std::expected`:

- `incorrect_type`: an item of another major type; a missing or another tag
  on a struct with a tag; a tag 29; a variant with no alternative that
  accepts the item; a plain array for a `std::vector` or `std::array` of a
  number type; an array of another count for a `std::tuple` or a
  `std::array`.
- `number_out_of_range`: an integer that the C++ type cannot hold; a
  binary64 that a `float` cannot hold exactly; a float under tag 1 whose
  value is not a whole number.
- `inadmissible_type_for_tag_content`: a tag 1 over an item that is not an
  integer or a float.
- `key_not_found`: a member that is not `std::optional` has no key in the
  map.
- `duplicate_key`: a key of a member, or a key of a set or a map, that comes
  twice.
- `too_little_data`: the input ends inside the item.
- `syntax_error`: a head is not well formed.
- `indefinite_length`: a head has indefinite length.
- `string_length_exceeded`, `container_elements_exceeded`,
  `nesting_depth_exceeded`: a string or a container is longer than a limit
  of the library, or the item nests too deep.
- `sharedref_index_not_marked`, `sharedref_not_complete`: see SHARED VALUES.
- `not_enough_memory`: a refused allocation of `%decode`, or a refusal of a
  `std::pmr` resource.
- `no_buffer_space`: a `std::span<char>` target that is too small.
- `value_too_large`: the size of the bytes does not fit in `std::size_t`.

These functions throw instead:

- `std::logic_error`: `%decode` with a null or empty owner; `encode` of a
  `std::chrono::year_month_day` that is not a valid date.
- `std::bad_alloc`: `encode` when the allocation of the result is refused.

@manonly
.SH "EXAMPLES"
@endmanonly
@snippet doc_reference_structs.cpp structs group
*/
